+# Cluster ci-00e6f05f41fa4f1a — build / windows-arm64 (SfzDistoBuffersTest's aligned `operator new`)
+
+## Verdict
+
+FIXED. The failing target is test infrastructure, and the bug is a real, deterministic
+compile failure on the hosted Windows-arm64 runner: the aligned allocation operators in
+`tests/src/plugins/SfzDistoBuffersTest.cpp` were guarded with `_MSC_VER`, but that runner
+compiles with clang on the mingw runtime (`MSYSTEM=CLANGARM64`), which defines `_WIN32`
+and does **not** define `_MSC_VER`. The test therefore fell through to the POSIX branch and
+called `posix_memalign`, which the Windows CRT does not declare:
+
+```
+tests/src/plugins/SfzDistoBuffersTest.cpp:60:6: error: use of undeclared identifier 'posix_memalign'
+```
+
+That is the exact diagnostic in the finding's CI log (run-43764792400-job-132967358917.log,
+commit 31f559ab479dcd1b822545aae477222f58f32c0d, the commit this worktree is on).
+
+## Evidence: where the wrong guard is and what the correct contract is
+
+- Before the fix: `tests/src/plugins/SfzDistoBuffersTest.cpp:10,56,82` used
+  `#if defined(_MSC_VER)` for the `<malloc.h>` include, the aligned `operator new`, and
+  the aligned `operator delete`. On clang/mingw all three are false, so the code included
+  no Windows header and called `posix_memalign` at line 59.
+- The sibling in the same test tree already had the correct contract:
+  `tests/src/core/AllocationProbe.cpp:31` (`#ifdef _WIN32` for `<malloc.h>`), `:124-131`
+  (`_aligned_malloc` on `_WIN32`, `posix_memalign` otherwise) and `:190-192`
+  (`_aligned_free` on `_WIN32`, `std::free` otherwise). This cluster makes
+  `SfzDistoBuffersTest` follow that same contract, so the allocation and its matching free
+  agree on every platform (a mismatched `_aligned_malloc`/`std::free` pair would corrupt the
+  Windows heap even if the build succeeded).
+- The fix: `tests/src/plugins/SfzDistoBuffersTest.cpp:11,59,87` now use
+  `#if defined(_WIN32)`. No behaviour changes on Linux (the POSIX branch is selected as
+  before) or on MSVC (MSVC defines `_WIN32` too). `_aligned_malloc`/`_aligned_free` are the
+  CRT contract on every Windows compiler, including clang/mingw.
+
+## Regression test (deterministic, no clock, no race)
+
+`tests/windows-arm64-aligned-new-check.py` reads the three marked platform blocks out of the
+real test source (markers `zene-portable-aligned-include/new/delete` in
+`tests/src/plugins/SfzDistoBuffersTest.cpp:10-14,53-67,83-93`) and compiles them twice with
+the host compiler:
+
+1. native (no `-D_WIN32`): the POSIX branch must allocate with the real `posix_memalign`
+   and free with `std::free`;
+2. simulated Windows (`-D_WIN32`, shim `malloc.h` declaring `_aligned_malloc`/`_aligned_free`
+   exactly as the Windows CRT does, `posix_memalign` poisoned to return ENOMEM): the
+   operators must select `_aligned_malloc`; if they fall through to `posix_memalign` the
+   probe's `operator new` throws `std::bad_alloc` and the check fails with
+   "the POSIX posix_memalign branch was selected".
+
+The shim only *declares* `_aligned_malloc`; its definition and the poison live after the
+extracted operators, so the extracted `#include <malloc.h>` guard is load-bearing too: a
+wrong include guard makes the probe fail to compile, exactly as the Windows build did.
+Registered as CTest `WindowsArm64AlignedNewCheck` in `tests/CMakeLists.txt:6237-6245`
+(Linux host, same pattern as the existing source-derived `Train*Portability` checks).
+
+Test matrix covered:
+- normal path: native `operator new(std::size_t, std::align_val_t)` returns 32-byte-aligned
+  storage and the matching delete frees it;
+- Windows path: `_WIN32` select the CRT helpers, and the aligned delete routes to
+  `_aligned_free` (the probe aborts if it does not);
+- failure/exhaustion: the poisoned POSIX entry point makes a wrong branch throw
+  `std::bad_alloc`, which is reported as a failure rather than passing silently;
+- platform-build requirement: the include/declaration path is compiled, not source-scanned.
+
